         r"E_{\text{cell}} = E^\circ_{\text{cell}} - \frac{RT}{nF}\ln Q = E^\circ_{\text{cell}} - \frac{0.0592\text{ V}}{n}\log Q \quad (\text{at } 25^\circ\text{C})",
         "Derived by substituting ΔG = −nFE and ΔG° = −nFE° into ΔG = ΔG° + RT ln Q (Walther Nernst, 1889): predicts how cell voltage changes with ion concentrations and gas partial pressures via the reaction quotient Q. As a battery discharges, Q increases until Q = K, at which point E_cell = 0 V ('dead battery'). Also explains concentration cells and glass pH electrodes (59.2 mV per pH unit at 25 °C).",
